<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.CommunityLife</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.CommunityLife</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Community Life, Events &amp; Organizations Platform (Phase B12). The platform's single owner
      of who gathers, where, and when.

      This is not an event calendar, and the distinction is load-bearing. A calendar answers "what is
      on". This module answers "who are my people here, and how do I find them" — which is why the
      Organization is a first-class aggregate rather than a field on an event, why membership is a
      workflow rather than a follow, and why an RSVP keeps its whole history rather than a single
      current value.

      What Community Life owns: organizations, organization memberships, events, RSVPs and their
      check-ins, community-life reports, the event and organization analytics rollups, and the
      organization-type and event-category catalogues. What it deliberately does not: the badge on an
      organization belongs to Trust, the neighbourhood belongs to Location and its profile to
      Housing, the photograph bytes belong to Media, the discussion under an event belongs to
      Community, the agency behind an organizer belongs to Providers, and the explainer beside a
      festival belongs to Guides. Community Life reaches every one of them through published
      contracts and stores identifiers, never copies.

      The two-aggregate split is the module's central claim. An Organization outlives every event it
      runs, has members who are not attendees, and is verified as an institution. An Event is a
      bounded occasion with a capacity, a start time and a guest list. Modelling either as the other
      collapses a real distinction: a church is not a service, and a service is not a church. They
      are related through an optional reference — an event may have an organizing organization, and
      most do not.

      Search is a seam, not an engine: the module ships an EF-backed search over the write model and
      the ICommunityEventSearchEngine / IOrganizationSearchEngine ports an OpenSearch adapter can
      satisfy later without a domain change. Organization types, event categories, ranking, spam
      signals, check-in methods, membership policies, view tracking, visibility and calendar sources
      are contributor seams on the same terms.

      Ticket payments, paid events, seat allocation, QR code generation, live streaming, video
      conferencing, sponsorship, AI recommendations, notification campaigns and calendar-provider
      integration are explicitly out of scope, and the abstractions here are shaped so each arrives
      as a new module or a new adapter rather than as a restructuring of this one.

      Registers entirely through B2.6 contributor interfaces: no edit to the Platform Kernel, the
      Identity module, Trust, Location, Media, Community, Providers, Guides, Jobs, Marketplace,
      Housing, or Program.cs.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    B11's reference set, plus Housing. Location, Media, Community, Providers, Guides and Housing are
    deliberate and documented. B12 consumes Location's published Integration and Extensibility
    contracts (IGeographicScopeResolver, INearbyResourceProvider, ILocationResourceCounter, and the
    administrative-area catalogue that owns the neighbourhood itself), Media's published reference
    contracts (attach-by-reference, never URLs — and both MediaOwnerType.Event and
    MediaOwnerType.Organization have existed since B5), Community's published Extensibility contract
    (ICommunityContentSource — and CommunityContentType.Event and .Organization have existed since
    B6), Providers' published IProviderDirectory for the agencies and institutions behind a host,
    Guides' published IGuideDirectory for the festival and etiquette explainers beside an event, and
    Housing's published INeighborhoodDirectory for the neighbourhood profile a local event is
    discovered through.

    Notably absent: the Identity module, the Trust module, the Jobs module and the Marketplace
    module. Community Life reaches Identity through the kernel's ICurrentUser and Trust through
    Diaspora.Contracts — the published ITrustVerificationSubmission contract and Trust's published
    integration events — never through their aggregates. Jobs and Marketplace are not referenced at
    all: they are sibling verticals, and an edge to either would be the first cycle in the graph
    B2.6 exists to keep acyclic. Career fairs and vendor booths are expressed as typed linked
    resources the organizer supplies, and "jobs near this event" is answered through Location's
    INearbyResourceProvider registry, which Jobs already contributes to and does not know this
    module reads. Architecture tests enforce all five.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

